dalone in terms of the couple, the siblings we follow, most of the world-building, and one over-arching line of action were introduced first in <a href="https://goodreads.com/book/show/55919400.Ruthless_King__Ruthless_Warlords__1_" title="Ruthless King (Ruthless Warlords #1) by Alison Aimes" rel="noopener">Ruthless King</a>.<br /><br />Here, we get second brother, control-freak Maxheim, and Tess, the slave he falls in love with. Although their attraction is very Omegaverse-instant, will Tess' close relationship with the man who nearly destroyed Maxheim's family tear them apart?<br /><br />I enjoyed this book and recommend it highly -- particularly if you like Omegaverse, but don't like how brutally Alphas treat their Omegas in many of them. Maxheim clearly adores Tess, will do anything for her, and never harms her. I also appreciate that Omegas have more agency in this series than they usually do; thus, Tess is equally appealing: her deprived childhood, low caste, and problematic "gift" make it difficult for her to trust Maxheim's feelings; once she does, it was a pleasure watching her come into her own -- finding happiness she never thought she'd have and a strength she didn't know she possessed. The MC, in fact, is this book's strength. Omegaverse is often saturated with long descriptions of animalistic sex (don't worry, some are present here), but, in order to invest in a couple, I need to see some other connection between them; this occurs here when each helps the other process past traumas in healthier ways. Finally (in terms of the couple), their conflict was organic, high-stakes, and seemingly insurmountable since it rests on competing loyalties -- which held this reader's interest throughout.<br /><br />The rest of the narrative was equally compelling: we find out more about the main mystery and the book ends with a couple of twists I didn't see coming. There are also a couple of poignant emotional moments that, while some were difficult to read, added depth to the usual Omegaverse/Mafia generic tropes.<br /><br />This would easily have been a five-star rating without the constant repetition that led to unnecessary length -- the same problem I had with the previous book. I wish that authors understood that readers really can retain information: I didn't, for example, need to be reminded of Tess's motivations quite so many times, read another scene of Maxheim's siblings dissecting his personality, or told so often that the family's position within the Brotherhood was difficult.<br /><br />4 stars. Third brother Alexi will get the next book. Note: The masks at the beginning have new meaning in the age of Covid.</span></div><div class=""></div></div></section></section><section class="ReviewCard__tags"><ul class="CollapsableList"><span tabindex="-1"><a href="https://www.goodreads.com/review/list/98512744?shelf=interesting" class="Button Button--tag Button--medium"><span class="Button__labelItem">interesting</span></a><span tabindex="-1"> </span><a href="https://www.goodreads.com/review/list/98512744?shelf=likeable-characters" class="Button Button--tag Button--medium"><span class="Button__labelItem">likeable-characters</span></a><span tabindex="-1"> </span><a href="https://www.goodreads.com/review/list/98512744?shelf=mutual-respect" class="Button Button--tag Button--medium"><span class="Button__labelItem">mutual-respect</span></a></span><span tabindex="-1"></span> <div class="Button__container"><button type="button" class="Button Button--tag Button--medium" aria-label="Show all items in the list"><span class="Button__labelItem">...more</span></button></div></ul></section><footer class="SocialFooter"><div data-testid="stats" class="SocialFooter__statsContainer"><div class="Button__container"><button type="button" class="Button Button--inline Button--medium Button--subdued"><span class="Button__labelItem">8 likes</span></button></div></div><div data-testid="actions"><div class="SocialFooter__action"><div class="Button__conta